index cda416b..cf99533 100644
--- a/docs/playing.md
+++ b/docs/playing.md
@@ -46,6 +46,8 @@ A controller macOS pairs but Apple's controller support does not list, such as a
 
 Two identical controllers that Apple's controller support lists as one, such as two Pro Controllers over USB, are each a player of their own: after 3 seconds the log says `GameController lists 1 of 2`, and each takes a player, by USB port. They have no vibration, player lights or Home button to quit. `DM_PAD_IDENTICAL=0` in `env.txt` turns this off.
 
+A wired Xbox 360 pad, or an Xbox One pad that is not Microsoft's (a Turtle Beach REACT-R, for example), has no driver on macOS and is not listed there at all: it is read directly over USB, takes a player as any other controller does, and the log says `xusb: Xbox 360 pad '<name>' is read directly over USB` or `xusb: Xbox One pad ...`. Only one app can read such a pad at a time, and it has no vibration, player light or Home button to quit. `DM_XUSB=0` in `env.txt` turns this off.
+
 With [Press a button to join](settings.md#press-a-button-to-join) on in the settings (`DM_PAD_PRESS_TO_JOIN=1` in `env.txt`), on the Mac and on iOS, a controller becomes a player only when one of its buttons is pressed, as the lowest player free: the players choose their order and how many play, over Bluetooth or USB, identical pads included, and a controller connected while the game runs joins at its first press. The log says `press a button to play`, then `a button pressed: player N`. The press that joins also reaches the game. When a game asks for more players than it sees, a prompt over the game says how many are wanted and joined and waits for the others: a controller that presses a button (or connects, with the setting off) joins, A finishes once enough are in, and B or Escape closes it, as on a Switch.
 
 To quit with a controller, hold its Home button (the logo button on an Xbox or PlayStation controller) for 2 seconds, then press A to quit or B to go back to the game. Quitting this way is the same as ⌘Q: progress the game has not saved is lost. If holding Home opens the Games app instead, macOS keeps the Home button for itself: its controller settings for the Home button decide whether apps may use it. `DM_HOME_QUIT=0` in `env.txt` turns the hold off. A short press of Home opens the Home menu over the paused game: up and down choose, A selects, B or Home goes back to the game. It has Resume and Quit (which asks first), and Back to console mode when the game was started from a launcher's console mode, which pauses the game in the background and shows the launcher. `DM_HOME_MENU=0` in `env.txt` turns the menu off.
